course: dictionary
lesson: 14518
title: Perception: noticing and understanding
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Perception is a formal word with three main meanings. First, it is noticing and understanding things with your senses: seeing, hearing, touching, tasting and smelling. Second, it is the way a person thinks about something, their view or impression. Third, it is being good at noticing things that others miss.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «Her perception of the sound told her the car was close.» Here the sentence is about hearing, so perception means noticing with the senses. «Public perception of the park changed after the clean-up.» Here the sentence is about what people think, so perception means their opinion.
explain: Ask yourself: is the sentence about the senses or about people's views? Then swap in your meaning and check that it still makes sense. Scientists use the word too: «Depth perception helps us judge how far away things are.»
fact: Perception means noticing and understanding things with your senses.
fact: Perception can also mean the way a person thinks about something, their view or impression.
fact: «Colour perception» is how your eyes and brain see and tell apart colours.
fact: «Depth perception» helps you judge how far away things are.
fact: Optical illusions trick our perception, so we can see something wrongly.
exercise: «The shapes in the picture tricked her perception, so the line looked longer.» What does perception mean here? | A kind of paint | A loud noise in the room | *The way her senses and brain understood the picture | The name of the artist
exercise: «Public perception of the park changed after the clean-up.» What does perception mean here? | How fast the park was cleaned | *The way people think about the park, their view of it | A map of the park's paths | The number of trees in the park
exercise: «Ben has great perception: he spotted the tiny crack in the glass before anyone else.» What does great perception mean for Ben? | Being very tired after a long day | Being able to sing very well | Having lots of money to spend | *Being good at noticing things that others miss
test: «Depth perception helps a bird judge how far away the branch is.» What does depth perception mean? | *Using the senses to judge how far away things are | Knowing the names of all the birds | A deep hole in the ground | Feeling sleepy in the afternoon
test: «Her perception of the new teacher was that she was kind and fair.» What does perception mean here? | A rule about homework | A test of her eyesight | *Her view or impression of the teacher | A kind of food
exam: «Optical illusions can trick our perception.» What is the best meaning of perception in this sentence? | The number of lines on a page | A type of camera | A printed list of colours | *The way our senses and brain understand what we see
